eral  in a kind of metaphorical shield against  distraction  if you try to read the same text on your  computer screen or on your phone you can  do it  and it's better than nothing but what  you find is  you you immediately have all these other  calls on your attention just  as if you were doing anything else on  your phone or a computer so there's  something  there's something special that it seems  like the  the the content of a book  is very very well suited to the printed  physical form in your ability to sustain  your attention for a long period of time  that's required to read  so i think you know the the death  sentence that was  that seemed to be looming over the  printed book 10 years ago it it hasn't  panned out  and i think you know you could argue  that that printed books are  less and less central to our culture um  compared to  the internet and social media but still  when people want to read they've shown a  distinct continuing preference for the  printed  form of that yeah almost beyond logic  right i mean it's like  i'm buying bookshelves and you know that  this is as you  you know you have a bunch of books  behind you they take up a lot of space  it's increasingly not logical to some  degree  um and yet right as a format it  perseveres  yeah yeah and i think there are other  reasons as well  i mean you know there's books behind me  and  what what you find is there is a tactile  there's something tactile about a  printed book that's really important  you know i can go back and grab one of  those books off off a shelf  and kind of in a kind of an amazing way  if i'm looking for a particular passage  that i read a long time ago  there's something you can find you can  actually find it pretty  quickly because there's some coming back  to the theme of muscle memory  there's some kind of muscle memory that  comes with the when you read a book  in a tactile way you're there's  i don't know what exactly it is but your  sense of touch  your sense of feel becomes wound up  in in the act of reading and there have  even been studies that show  that that translates into better  retention  and better compreh reading comprehension  um  uh studies of students for instance  show that they retain more and they  comprehend more when they read and print  than when they read the same uh content  online  i've seen yeah that's that's uh that's  super interesting we've been speaking  with nicholas carr author of several  books  on technology and culture including the  pulitzer prize finalists the shallows  what the internet is doing to our brains  i so appreciate your time today thank  you thank you  my pleasure
 

